        {
          "say": "It works for any convex polygon. 3 sides or 100 sides.",
          "write": "any n",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y8/maths/exterior-angles-turns.svg",
          "alt": "Quadrilateral with four exterior angles a, b, c, d"
        },
        {
          "say": "Convex means every inside angle is less than 180.",
          "write": "convex"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Why it's 360",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "At n corners, interior plus exterior gives n times 180.",
          "write": "n × 180"
        },
        {
          "say": "The interior angles use n minus 2, times 180.",
          "write": "(n − 2) × 180"
        },
        {
          "say": "What's left is 2 times 180. That's 360.",
          "write": "n×180 − (n−2)×180 = 360"
        },
        {
          "say": "The sum doesn't depend on n at all."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Regular polygons",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "In a regular polygon, all the exterior angles are equal.",
          "write": "each = 360° ÷ n"
        },
        {
          "say": "A regular octagon: 360 divided by 8 is 45 degrees.",
          "write": "360 ÷ 8 = 45°",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y8/maths/exterior-angles-ex1.svg",
          "alt": "Regular octagon with one exterior angle marked"
        },
        {
          "say": "So each interior angle is 180 minus 45. That's 135.",
          "write": "180 − 45 = 135°"
        },
        {
          "say": "A decagon: 36 outside, 144 inside.",
          "write": "360 ÷ 10 = 36°, 144°"
        },
        {
          "say": "More sides means smaller turns.",
          "write": "triangle 120°, 36-gon 10°"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Finding the number of sides",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Each exterior angle is 15 degrees. How many sides?",
          "write": "n = 360 ÷ 15 = 24"
        },
        {
          "say": "Interior angle 140? Change to exterior first. 180 minus 140 is 40.",
          "write": "exterior = 40°",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 300,
            "alt": "Interior 140 becomes exterior 40",
            "items": [
              {
                "kind": "line",
                "x": 40,
                "y": 220,
                "x2": 520,
                "y2": 220,
                "color": "black"
              },
              {
                "kind": "line",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 220,
                "x2": 120,
                "y2": 90,
                "color": "blue"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 330,
                "y": 190,
                "text": "140°",
                "size": 22,
                "color": "blue",
                "anchor": "middle",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 175,
                "y": 208,
                "text": "40°",
                "size": 22,
                "color": "green",
                "anchor": "middle",
                "bold": true,
                "change": "add"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 275,
                "text": "interior + exterior = 180°",
                "size": 20,
                "color": "purple",
                "anchor": "middle"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "Then 360 divided by 40 is 9 sides.",
          "write": "n = 9"
        },
        {
          "say": "Don't do 360 divided by 140. It's not a whole number.",
          "write": "✗ 360 ÷ 140"
        },
        {
          "say": "50 degrees? 360 divided by 50 is 7.2. No such regular polygon.",
          "write": "✗ 7.2 sides"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "A missing exterior angle",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "A pentagon's exterior angles are 70, 80, 65, 75 and x.",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y8/maths/exterior-angles-ex4.svg",
          "alt": "Pentagon with exterior angles 70°, 80°, 65°, 75°, x°"
        },
        {
          "say": "The known ones add to 290.",
          "write": "70 + 80 + 65 + 75 = 290"
        },
        {
          "say": "So x is 360 minus 290, which is 70.",
          "write": "x = 70"
        },
        {
          "say": "The interior angle there is 180 minus 70. That's 110.",
          "write": "interior = 110°"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Remember",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Walk the park, turn a circle. Exterior angles add to 360.",
          "write": "360°"
        },
        {
          "say": "Interior plus exterior is 180 at every corner.",
          "write": "180°"
        },
        {
          "say": "Regular: 360 divided by n gives each exterior angle.",
          "write": "360 ÷ n"
        },
        {
          "say": "Need sides? Change to exterior first, then divide into 360.",
          "write": "n = 360 ÷ ext"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
